From: well0nez <well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] soc: sunxi: give the H713 cpu_comm IPC an in-kernel API

The MIPS co-processor of this SoC owns the HDMI receiver and the whole
picture pipeline; everything it can do is a THal_Vp_* remote procedure
over the shared-memory IPC this driver implements. Until now the only way
to reach it was the /dev/cpu_comm character device, so the drivers that
actually need the co-processor -- the HDMI-RX V4L2 driver, the ARISC HDMI
driver -- would have had to be driven from a userspace daemon, which is
also where the callbacks the co-processor sends back would have to be
turned around and pushed into the kernel again.

Add the two calls those drivers need:

    int cpu_comm_call(comp_id, args, nargs, ret, nret, timeout_ms);
    int cpu_comm_register_callback(name, fn, ctx);

plus cpu_comm_unregister_callback() and cpu_comm_name2id(), in
<linux/soc/sunxi/h713-cpu-comm.h>.

Four things are worth saying about the shape of it.

The timeout is real. CPUComm_CallEx waits 500 ms for the RETURN and then
carries on regardless, because GetReturnbySessionId reports "no matching
session" as success -- so a co-processor that never answered is
indistinguishable from a routine that returns nothing. That is the stock
semantic and IOCTL_CALL keeps it; a kernel caller gets -ETIMEDOUT
instead. The call engine is the same function in both cases, with the
policy passed in, so there is one implementation of the protocol and not
two. The 32-bit result words are still read the way that function
already reads them: the shared region is an ioremap() mapping, and a
64-bit load from a 4-byte-aligned address in it is an alignment fault.

Callback delivery is added next to the existing one, not instead of it.
command_action() already hands every incoming call to each open
/dev/cpu_comm fd; one line below, it now also hands it to a registered
kernel handler. Both see the same 104-byte message and neither can
inhibit the other or the acknowledgement. That point is above the split
between the FIFO path (channel field <= 4) and the workqueue path (> 4),
so a handler sees both classes of callback exactly once -- which matters
because which class a callback falls into is not something to rely on:
every SignalChange and HdmiHotPlugByPortHandler captured on the bench so
far arrived with channel field 0, the FIFO path.

Registering a handler also registers a routine, and that is the half an
earlier version of this patch left out. The hook point above is
independent of the routine table, which is true and says nothing about
the other end of the link: the firmware resolves a MipsHalCallback_* by
its descriptor in the routine table in shared memory. SendComm2CPUEx --
the same function this driver ports for the ARM -- fails the FindRoutine
lookup with -3 before it touches the message FIFO, so a callback nobody
installed a descriptor for is never put on the wire. The handler then
never runs and its counter stays at zero while the co-processor's own log
shows the callback firing, which is exactly what the HDMI-RX driver saw.
Every incoming call captured on the bench carries the proof that the
lookup does happen: the message's +0x10 word is the descriptor's owner
field, copied there from routine_find_buf + 4 by SendComm2CPUEx step 14,
and that field exists nowhere but in the table.

So cpu_comm_register_callback() installs the descriptor the way
IOCTL_INSTALL_RT does, and takes the routine's name rather than its id --
the descriptor carries the name, and the id is only its hash, which there
is no way back from. The owner field is 0: it has to be a value no task
can carry, because cpu_comm_release() hands the closing task's pid to
RemovePidRoutines(), and it has to keep the derived channel key below
0x10000, because Comm_Add2NewCallFifo() checks the key's upper half
against the call entry. Unregistering removes the descriptor again, but
only when it is still the one this API installed, so a userspace daemon
that registered the same routine keeps its own entry -- and only when the
lookup in front of it says the entry's cpu field is 0, because
RemoveRoutine() has two arms that return while still holding hardware
spinlock 2 and this is its first in-kernel caller. The other arm, an
out-of-range chain link, is already excluded by the same lookup having
walked that chain.

Registering has an immediate effect on the other processor, and that is a
rule for callers rather than an implementation detail, so it is written
into the header: the descriptor is what arms the sender, and the first
call can arrive as soon as cpu_comm_register_callback() returns, on the
receive work item and in parallel with whatever the registering driver
does next. A driver registers when it is ready to be called, not at the
top of a probe that then runs a chain of outgoing calls. The HDMI-RX
driver did the latter, and one single delivered callback landing inside
that chain broke its probe in two different places on two cold starts.

The broken kernel-callback mechanism goes at the same time.
comm_CallWorkAction() read a function pointer out of routine_info + 88 and
jumped to it, but FindRoutineEx never fills bytes 88..95, so the target was
whatever was on the stack -- and even at the offset the vendor really uses,
that table holds MIPS handler addresses and userspace-registered names, never
an ARM kernel function pointer. Nothing there was ever worth calling. Now that
kernel-side receipt has a mechanism of its own, the jump is removed rather than
guarded; the acknowledgement the co-processor waits for stays exactly as it
was, which is also how the module that ran on the bench behaved (there the
jump was disabled by a module parameter).

Outgoing calls from the kernel and from IOCTL_CALL take the same mutex.
Uncontended that costs nothing and leaves the ioctl behaving exactly as
before; what it prevents is two calls interleaving inside SendComm2CPUEx,
whose own per-sequence semaphore gives up after 100 ms and proceeds
anyway. Now that there can be a second caller, that guard is not enough
on its own.

Routines are addressed by a 32-bit id, and the id turns out to be
derivable after all: a CRC-32 (0xedb88320, LSB-first, no inversion) over
the full routine name, seeded with 0x00123456 -- the vendor's
Trid_Util_Name2ID. It reproduces every named entry of the dumped call
table, the two with the vendor's inconsistent lower-case "Thal_"
included, so cpu_comm_name2id("THal_Vp_SetSource", 1) is 0xeaf13de5 and
no table has to be carried in the driver.

A debugfs consumer of both halves comes with it, so the path can be
exercised on the bench without a userspace program:

    echo THal_Vp_SetSource 3           > /sys/kernel/debug/cpu_comm/call
    cat                                  /sys/kernel/debug/cpu_comm/call
    echo MipsHalCallback_SignalChange   > /sys/kernel/debug/cpu_comm/watch
    cat                                  /sys/kernel/debug/cpu_comm/watch

Reading "watch" is also the instrument for the failure above. It counts
every incoming call that reached the delivery hook, whether or not
anything was registered for it, counts the ones no handler claimed, names
the last of those, and prints for each handler what the routine table
really says about its id. "rt none" on a line is the state in which the
firmware cannot find the receiver; rx_calls staying at zero while the
firmware logs the callback says the call is not on the wire at all. A
per-driver event counter cannot tell those two apart, which is how this
took a night to find.

The character devices, the ioctls and the shared-memory protocol are
otherwise untouched.

Signed-off-by: well0nez <well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
---
diff -ruN a/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_api.c b/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_api.c
--- a/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_api.c	1970-01-01 01:00:00.000000000 +0100
+++ b/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_api.c	2026-09-07 09:41:19.818544878 +0200
@@ -0,0 +1,871 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+/*
+ * cpu_comm_api.c -- in-kernel API for the ARM <-> MIPS IPC.
+ *
+ * Three things live here:
+ *
+ *   cpu_comm_call()			a synchronous THal_Vp_* call with an
+ *					honest timeout,
+ *   cpu_comm_register_callback()	kernel-side receipt of the calls the
+ *					MIPS makes back into the ARM,
+ *   a debugfs consumer of both		so the path can be exercised on the
+ *					bench without a userspace program.
+ *
+ * The character devices are not touched. Outgoing calls from here and
+ * from IOCTL_CALL take the same mutex, so a kernel driver and a userspace
+ * tool cannot interleave two calls inside SendComm2CPUEx -- which they
+ * would otherwise do, because the per-sequence semaphore in there gives up
+ * after 100 ms and continues anyway.
+ *
+ * Callback delivery is hooked in command_action() (cpu_comm_proto.c), one
+ * line below the existing cpu_comm_userspace_deliver(). Order is: every
+ * open /dev/cpu_comm fd first, then the kernel handler. Both see the same
+ * 104-byte message; neither can inhibit the other or the acknowledgement
+ * that follows. That hook point is before the split between the FIFO path
+ * (channel field <= 4) and the workqueue path (> 4), so a kernel handler
+ * sees both classes exactly once. Which class a given callback belongs to
+ * is not a thing to rely on: every MipsHalCallback_SignalChange and
+ * MipsHalCallback_HdmiHotPlugByPortHandler captured on the bench so far
+ * arrived with channel field 0, the FIFO path, contrary to what doku/72
+ * assumed about SignalChange.
+ *
+ * That hook point being independent of the routine table is what made an
+ * earlier version of this file believe the table did not matter. It does,
+ * on the other side of the link: registering a handler here does not make
+ * the co-processor send anything. The firmware resolves MipsHalCallback_*
+ * through the routine table in shared memory -- SendComm2CPUEx() calls
+ * FindRoutine() and returns -3 when the id is not in the table, before it
+ * touches the message FIFO -- so a callback with no descriptor is never
+ * put on the wire, and the hook never runs. Every incoming call captured
+ * on the bench proves the lookup happened: the message carries the
+ * descriptor's owner field back at +0x10, copied from routine_find_buf+4
+ * by SendComm2CPUEx() step 14, and that field only exists in the table.
+ * cpu_comm_register_callback() therefore installs the descriptor as
+ * IOCTL_INSTALL_RT does, and takes the routine's name to build it.
+ *
+ * Which makes registering an act with an immediate effect on the other
+ * processor, and that is a rule for callers rather than a detail of this
+ * file: from the moment the descriptor is in the table the MIPS can send,
+ * and delivery runs on the receive work item, in parallel with whatever
+ * the registering driver does next. The HDMI-RX driver registered at the
+ * top of its probe and then ran twenty-two outgoing calls plus the ARISC
+ * EDID sequence; on 07.09. exactly one delivered callback landed inside
+ * that chain and broke the probe, in a different place on each of two
+ * cold starts. Register when ready to be called -- see the header.
+ */
+
+#include <linux/build_bug.h>
+#include <linux/crc32.h>
+#include <linux/debugfs.h>
+#include <linux/io.h>
+#include <linux/kernel.h>
+#include <linux/mutex.h>
+#include <linux/slab.h>
+#include <linux/stddef.h>
+#include <linux/string.h>
+#include <linux/uaccess.h>
+
+#include <linux/soc/sunxi/h713-cpu-comm.h>
+
+#include "cpu_comm.h"
+
+/* ── Routine name -> component id ───────────────────────────── */
+
+/*
+ * The vendor's Trid_Util_Name2ID (libUtility.so @0x1DC61) is a CRC-32 with
+ * the usual reflected 0xedb88320 polynomial, no pre- or post-inversion,
+ * seeded with 0x00123456 instead of ~0 -- which is exactly what the
+ * kernel's crc32_le() computes. The string it hashes is the full routine
+ * name including the "_<cpu>_<pid>" suffix; every stock routine uses pid 0.
+ */
+/*
+ * Spell out the name that is hashed and that goes into the routine
+ * descriptor: "<base>_<cpu>_<pid>", pid 0 for every stock routine.
+ *
+ * Return: the length written, or -EINVAL if it does not fit @out_len.
+ */
+static int cpu_comm_full_name(char *out, size_t out_len, const char *base_name,
+			      unsigned int target_cpu)
+{
+	int len;
+
+	if (!base_name || !*base_name)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	len = snprintf(out, out_len, "%s_%1x_%3.3x",
+		       base_name, target_cpu & 0xf, 0);
+	if (len <= 0 || len >= (int)out_len)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	return len;
+}
+
+u32 cpu_comm_name2id(const char *base_name, unsigned int target_cpu)
+{
+	char name[CPU_COMM_NAME_MAX + 8];
+	int len;
+
+	len = cpu_comm_full_name(name, sizeof(name), base_name, target_cpu);
+	if (len < 0)
+		return 0;
+
+	return crc32_le(CPU_COMM_NAME2ID_SEED, name, len);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(cpu_comm_name2id);
+
+/* ── Outgoing calls ─────────────────────────────────────────── */
+
+DEFINE_MUTEX(cpu_comm_call_mutex);
+
+/*
+ * SendComm2CPUEx and CPUComm_CallEx return the vendor's error numbers.
+ * Two of them are actively dangerous to hand out unchanged: -1 is
+ * -EPERM and -512 is -ERESTARTSYS, and neither means what it says.
+ * IOCTL_CALL hides all of this behind a flat -EFAULT; a kernel caller
+ * wants to be able to tell "not there yet" from "gone".
+ */
+static int cpu_comm_map_err(int err)
+{
+	switch (err) {
+	case -1:	/* isCPUAppReady() == 0: co-processor still starting */
+		return -EAGAIN;
+	case -3:	/* FindRoutine() found no entry for this comp_id */
+		return -ENODEV;
+	case -512:	/* IsCPUReset(): the co-processor is held in reset */
+		return -ENODEV;
+	default:
+		return err;
+	}
+}
+
+int cpu_comm_call(u32 comp_id, const u32 *args, unsigned int nargs,
+		  u32 *ret, unsigned int nret, unsigned int timeout_ms)
+{
+	int params[CPU_COMM_MAX_ARGS + 1];
+	u32 result[CPU_COMM_MAX_ARGS + 1];
+	unsigned int i, count;
+	int err;
+
+	if (nargs > CPU_COMM_MAX_ARGS || nret > CPU_COMM_MAX_ARGS)
+		return -EINVAL;
+	if ((nargs && !args) || (nret && !ret))
+		return -EINVAL;
+	if (!ShMemAddrBase || !pcpu_comm_dev)
+		return -ENODEV;
+
+	memset(params, 0, sizeof(params));
+	memset(result, 0, sizeof(result));
+
+	/* params[0] is the count, the values follow -- the layout the
+	 * vendor wrapper and IOCTL_CALL both use. */
+	params[0] = (int)nargs;
+	for (i = 0; i < nargs; i++)
+		params[i + 1] = (int)args[i];
+
+	mutex_lock(&cpu_comm_call_mutex);
+	err = cpu_comm_call_ex((int)comp_id, params, result, timeout_ms, true);
+	mutex_unlock(&cpu_comm_call_mutex);
+
+	if (err)
+		return cpu_comm_map_err(err);
+
+	if (nret) {
+		memset(ret, 0, nret * sizeof(*ret));
+		count = min_t(unsigned int, result[0], nret);
+		for (i = 0; i < count; i++)
+			ret[i] = result[i + 1];
+	} else {
+		count = 0;
+	}
+
+	return (int)count;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(cpu_comm_call);
+
+/* ── Incoming calls: the routine table ──────────────────────── */
+
+/*
+ * The 96-byte routine descriptor, exactly as IOCTL_INSTALL_RT takes it
+ * from userspace and as AddInRoutine() copies it into the table in shared
+ * memory. The field names are the vendor's (cpucomm.h); the layout is
+ * pinned by what the rest of this driver reads back out of it:
+ * FindRoutineEx() copies +0, +2, +4, +8, +12..75 and +80, and
+ * SendComm2CPUEx() puts the +4 word into every outgoing call at +0x10.
+ */
+struct cpu_comm_routine_desc {
+	u16	channel;	/* +0x00 low nibble is the channel key */
+	u16	target_cpu;	/* +0x02 CPU that implements the routine */
+	u32	owner;		/* +0x04 owner tag, echoed into every call */
+	u32	comp_id;	/* +0x08 hash of the full routine name */
+	char	name[64];	/* +0x0c */
+	u8	reserved[16];	/* +0x4c */
+	s32	next_index;	/* +0x5c hash-chain link */
+};
+static_assert(sizeof(struct cpu_comm_routine_desc) == 96,
+	      "routine descriptor must match a shared-table entry");
+
+/*
+ * What goes into the owner field, and why it is zero.
+ *
+ * Userspace writes its pid there. cpu_comm_release() then hands the
+ * closing task's pid to RemovePidRoutines(), which drops every entry
+ * carrying it, so the kernel's entries must be tagged with something no
+ * task can ever be: pid 0 belongs to the idle task, which never opens a
+ * file, so no close() can take these entries away.
+ *
+ * The tag comes back in every incoming call at +0x10, where
+ * Comm_Add2NewCallFifo() turns it into the channel key
+ * CPU_COMM_CPU_MIPS | owner << 4 -- one key for every kernel callback,
+ * registered once. That is the second reason for zero and the reason a
+ * value above PID_MAX_LIMIT does not work: the same function then checks
+ * the channel's own fields against the call entry, and the first of the
+ * three reads the key's upper 16 bits (Comm_AddNewChannel() stores the
+ * whole key in one word, so channel + 2 is key >> 16) against the call
+ * entry's channel field. That only holds while the key stays below
+ * 0x10000, which for a pid-derived key means a pid below 4096 -- an
+ * accident the character-device path lives on. Zero makes it exact
+ * rather than lucky: key 1, upper half 0, and 1 is a key no
+ * IOCTL_INSTALL_RT can produce (theirs is 1 | pid << 4, never below 17).
+ */
+#define CPU_COMM_KERNEL_OWNER	0u
+#define CPU_COMM_KERNEL_CHANNEL	(CPU_COMM_CPU_MIPS | (CPU_COMM_KERNEL_OWNER << 4))
+
+/* The channel pool, spelled the way the ioctl path spells it. */
+#define cpu_comm_channel_pool()	((u8 *)pcpu_comm_dev + 0x30)
+
+/*
+ * @base_name may be NULL when the descriptor is only used as a key, as it
+ * is for RemoveRoutine(). When it is given, the name field gets the same
+ * *full* name that was hashed, suffix included, because that is what
+ * IOCTL_INSTALL_RT puts there and the descriptor is read by the other
+ * processor.
+ */
+static int cpu_comm_fill_desc(struct cpu_comm_routine_desc *desc, u32 comp_id,
+			      const char *base_name)
+{
+	memset(desc, 0, sizeof(*desc));
+	desc->channel = 0;
+	desc->target_cpu = CPU_COMM_CPU_ARM;
+	desc->owner = CPU_COMM_KERNEL_OWNER;
+	desc->comp_id = comp_id;
+	desc->next_index = -1;
+
+	if (base_name && cpu_comm_full_name(desc->name, sizeof(desc->name),
+					    base_name, CPU_COMM_CPU_ARM) < 0)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * cpu_comm_routine_owner -- what the shared table says about @comp_id.
+ *
+ * Return: 0 and fill @owner/@target_cpu if there is an entry, -ENOENT if
+ * there is none, -ENODEV if the shared region is not mapped yet.
+ */
+static int cpu_comm_routine_owner(u32 comp_id, u32 *owner, u16 *target_cpu)
+{
+	u8 buf[96] __aligned(8);
+
+	if (!ShMemAddrBase)
+		return -ENODEV;
+
+	if (FindRoutineEx(comp_id, buf, NULL))
+		return -ENOENT;
+
+	*owner = *(u32 *)(buf + 4);
+	*target_cpu = *(u16 *)(buf + 2);
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * cpu_comm_install_routine -- put @name where the co-processor looks.
+ *
+ * Without this the firmware's SendComm2CPUEx() fails the FindRoutine()
+ * lookup for @comp_id and returns -3 before it touches the message FIFO:
+ * the callback is never sent, and no amount of handler registration on
+ * this side changes that. Same two steps as IOCTL_INSTALL_RT, for the
+ * same reason.
+ */
+static int cpu_comm_install_routine(u32 comp_id, const char *name)
+{
+	struct cpu_comm_routine_desc desc;
+	unsigned long existing = 0;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (!ShMemAddrBase || !pcpu_comm_dev)
+		return -ENODEV;
+
+	ret = cpu_comm_fill_desc(&desc, comp_id, name);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	ret = AddInRoutine(&desc);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret == -2 ? -ENOSPC : ret;
+
+	/*
+	 * AddInRoutine() treats an id that is already in the table as
+	 * success and leaves that entry alone -- one a userspace daemon
+	 * installed, or one it left behind. Either is fine here: what
+	 * matters is that the co-processor can resolve the name, and
+	 * delivery below does not care whose owner tag the entry carries.
+	 * The debugfs listing prints the owner that is really in the table
+	 * so the difference stays visible.
+	 */
+
+	if (Comm_QueryChannel(cpu_comm_channel_pool(), CPU_COMM_KERNEL_CHANNEL,
+			      &existing) == 0 && !existing)
+		Comm_AddNewChannel(cpu_comm_channel_pool(), CPU_COMM_CPU_MIPS,
+				   CPU_COMM_KERNEL_OWNER);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * The reverse. Only an entry this API installed is removed: a daemon that
+ * registered the same routine keeps its own, and a stale entry left by a
+ * dead process is not ours to clean up here.
+ *
+ * The channel stays registered. It is shared by every kernel callback,
+ * costs one of 32 pool slots, and the character-device path does not
+ * remove its channels on close either.
+ *
+ * The lookup in front of RemoveRoutine() is not only an ownership test.
+ * RemoveRoutine() has two arms that return without releasing hardware
+ * spinlock 2 -- an out-of-range chain link, and a found entry whose cpu
+ * field is above 1 (cpu_comm_rpc.c) -- and this is the first caller of it
+ * in the kernel, so leaving that reachable would mean a failed teardown
+ * could wedge the routine table for the co-processor as well as for
+ * IOCTL_INSTALL_RT. Both arms are ruled out here rather than there,
+ * because cpu_comm_rpc.c belongs to the base port:
+ *
+ *   - the chain: FindRoutineEx() has just walked the same chain to the
+ *     same entry, and it bails out on a link above RT_MAX_INDEX before
+ *     following it, so every link on the way is in range;
+ *   - the cpu field: it comes back from that same lookup and is checked
+ *     below, so RemoveRoutine() cannot meet a value it refuses.
+ */
+static void cpu_comm_remove_routine(u32 comp_id)
+{
+	struct cpu_comm_routine_desc desc;
+	u16 target_cpu = 0xffff;
+	u32 owner = 0;
+
+	if (cpu_comm_routine_owner(comp_id, &owner, &target_cpu) ||
+	    owner != CPU_COMM_KERNEL_OWNER)
+		return;
+
+	if (target_cpu != CPU_COMM_CPU_ARM) {
+		pr_warn("cpu_comm: routine 0x%08x carries cpu %u, left in the table\n",
+			comp_id, target_cpu);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	if (cpu_comm_fill_desc(&desc, comp_id, NULL))
+		return;
+	RemoveRoutine(&desc);
+}
+
+/* ── Incoming calls: kernel handlers ────────────────────────── */
+
+#define CPU_COMM_MAX_KERNEL_CB	16
+
+struct cpu_comm_kcb {
+	u32		comp_id;
+	cpu_comm_cb_t	fn;
+	void		*ctx;
+	unsigned long	hits;
+	char		name[CPU_COMM_NAME_MAX];
+};
+
+static struct cpu_comm_kcb cpu_comm_kcbs[CPU_COMM_MAX_KERNEL_CB];
+static DEFINE_MUTEX(cpu_comm_kcb_mutex);
+
+/*
+ * Instrumentation. @cpu_comm_rx_calls counts every incoming call that
+ * reached this file, whether or not anything was registered for it, and
+ * @cpu_comm_rx_unmatched counts the ones no handler claimed. The pair is
+ * what tells "the co-processor sends nothing" (rx stays 0) apart from
+ * "it sends and we drop it" (rx rises, unmatched rises with it) -- the
+ * two failure modes that look identical in a per-driver event counter.
+ */
+static atomic_t cpu_comm_rx_calls = ATOMIC_INIT(0);
+static atomic_t cpu_comm_rx_unmatched = ATOMIC_INIT(0);
+static u32 cpu_comm_rx_last_unmatched;	/* under cpu_comm_kcb_mutex */
+
+/* How many slots are in use; only read for the debugfs listing. */
+static atomic_t cpu_comm_kcb_count = ATOMIC_INIT(0);
+
+static struct cpu_comm_kcb *cpu_comm_kcb_find(u32 comp_id)
+{
+	int i;
+
+	lockdep_assert_held(&cpu_comm_kcb_mutex);
+
+	for (i = 0; i < CPU_COMM_MAX_KERNEL_CB; i++)
+		if (cpu_comm_kcbs[i].fn && cpu_comm_kcbs[i].comp_id == comp_id)
+			return &cpu_comm_kcbs[i];
+	return NULL;
+}
+
+int cpu_comm_register_callback(const char *base_name, cpu_comm_cb_t fn,
+			       void *ctx)
+{
+	char full[sizeof_field(struct cpu_comm_routine_desc, name)];
+	struct cpu_comm_kcb *slot = NULL;
+	u32 comp_id;
+	int i, ret;
+
+	if (!base_name || !fn)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	/*
+	 * The full name goes into the descriptor's 64-byte name field, so
+	 * the base name has to leave room for the "_<cpu>_<pid>" suffix
+	 * and the terminator. Refuse rather than hand the firmware a name
+	 * it would see truncated.
+	 */
+	if (cpu_comm_full_name(full, sizeof(full), base_name,
+			       CPU_COMM_CPU_ARM) < 0)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	comp_id = cpu_comm_name2id(base_name, CPU_COMM_CPU_ARM);
+	if (!comp_id)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	mutex_lock(&cpu_comm_kcb_mutex);
+
+	if (cpu_comm_kcb_find(comp_id)) {
+		ret = -EBUSY;
+		goto out;
+	}
+
+	for (i = 0; i < CPU_COMM_MAX_KERNEL_CB; i++) {
+		if (!cpu_comm_kcbs[i].fn) {
+			slot = &cpu_comm_kcbs[i];
+			break;
+		}
+	}
+	if (!slot) {
+		ret = -ENOSPC;
+		goto out;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Routine first: if the co-processor cannot be told about the
+	 * name, a handler for it would be a handler that can never run,
+	 * and the caller is better off seeing the error.
+	 */
+	ret = cpu_comm_install_routine(comp_id, base_name);
+	if (ret)
+		goto out;
+
+	slot->comp_id = comp_id;
+	slot->ctx = ctx;
+	slot->hits = 0;
+	strscpy(slot->name, base_name, sizeof(slot->name));
+	/* fn last: delivery treats it as the slot's valid marker. */
+	slot->fn = fn;
+	atomic_inc(&cpu_comm_kcb_count);
+	ret = 0;
+
+	pr_info("cpu_comm: kernel handler registered for comp=0x%08x %s\n",
+		comp_id, base_name);
+out:
+	mutex_unlock(&cpu_comm_kcb_mutex);
+	return ret;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(cpu_comm_register_callback);
+
+int cpu_comm_unregister_callback(const char *base_name, cpu_comm_cb_t fn)
+{
+	struct cpu_comm_kcb *slot;
+	u32 comp_id;
+	int ret = -ENOENT;
+
+	if (!base_name)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	comp_id = cpu_comm_name2id(base_name, CPU_COMM_CPU_ARM);
+	if (!comp_id)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	/* Taking the same mutex that delivery holds around the handler
+	 * call is what makes it safe to free the context afterwards. */
+	mutex_lock(&cpu_comm_kcb_mutex);
+
+	slot = cpu_comm_kcb_find(comp_id);
+	if (slot && (!fn || slot->fn == fn)) {
+		memset(slot, 0, sizeof(*slot));
+		atomic_dec(&cpu_comm_kcb_count);
+		cpu_comm_remove_routine(comp_id);
+		ret = 0;
+	}
+
+	mutex_unlock(&cpu_comm_kcb_mutex);
+	return ret;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(cpu_comm_unregister_callback);
+
+/*
+ * cpu_comm_kernel_deliver -- hand an incoming MIPS->ARM call to the
+ * registered kernel handler, if there is one.
+ *
+ * @msg points into the shared region, which is an ioremap() mapping, so
+ * the copy goes through memcpy_fromio() rather than memcpy().
+ *
+ * Called from command_action() in process context (the receive work item),
+ * right after the message has been offered to the character devices.
+ *
+ * The message is copied and counted even when nothing is registered: the
+ * counters are the instrument that says whether calls arrive at all, and
+ * an instrument that only works once the thing it measures is already
+ * working is no instrument.
+ */
+void cpu_comm_kernel_deliver(const void *msg)
+{
+	u8 local[COMM_MSG_SIZE] __aligned(8);
+	u32 args[CPU_COMM_MAX_ARGS];
+	struct cpu_comm_kcb *slot;
+	unsigned int nargs, i;
+	u32 comp_id;
+
+	if (!msg)
+		return;
+
+	memcpy_fromio(local, (__force const void __iomem *)msg, COMM_MSG_SIZE);
+
+	comp_id = *(u32 *)(local + 40);		/* +0x28 */
+	nargs = *(u16 *)(local + 8);		/* +0x08 ParaCount */
+	if (nargs > CPU_COMM_MAX_ARGS)
+		nargs = CPU_COMM_MAX_ARGS;
+	for (i = 0; i < nargs; i++)
+		args[i] = *(u32 *)(local + 44 + 4 * i);	/* +0x2c.. */
+
+	atomic_inc(&cpu_comm_rx_calls);
+
+	mutex_lock(&cpu_comm_kcb_mutex);
+	slot = cpu_comm_kcb_find(comp_id);
+	if (slot) {
+		slot->hits++;
+		slot->fn(comp_id, nargs ? args : NULL, nargs, slot->ctx);
+	} else {
+		cpu_comm_rx_last_unmatched = comp_id;
+		atomic_inc(&cpu_comm_rx_unmatched);
+	}
+	mutex_unlock(&cpu_comm_kcb_mutex);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Module teardown. The descriptors have to go with the handlers: an entry
+ * left behind points the co-processor at a receiver that is no longer
+ * there, and the next module to load would find the id already taken.
+ */
+static void cpu_comm_drop_all_handlers(void)
+{
+	int i;
+
+	mutex_lock(&cpu_comm_kcb_mutex);
+	for (i = 0; i < CPU_COMM_MAX_KERNEL_CB; i++) {
+		if (!cpu_comm_kcbs[i].fn)
+			continue;
+		cpu_comm_remove_routine(cpu_comm_kcbs[i].comp_id);
+	}
+	memset(cpu_comm_kcbs, 0, sizeof(cpu_comm_kcbs));
+	atomic_set(&cpu_comm_kcb_count, 0);
+	mutex_unlock(&cpu_comm_kcb_mutex);
+}
+
+/* ── debugfs: a consumer of both halves ─────────────────────── */
+
+#ifdef CONFIG_DEBUG_FS
+
+static struct dentry *cpu_comm_debug_dir;
+static char cpu_comm_debug_result[192];
+static DEFINE_MUTEX(cpu_comm_debug_mutex);
+
+/*
+ * "THal_Vp_SetSource", "THal_Vp_SetSource@1" or "0xeaf13de5" -- a routine
+ * name with an optional CPU suffix, or a raw component id. The default
+ * CPU is 1 (MIPS), which is what every THal_Vp_* routine wants;
+ * MipsHalCallback_* routines live on the ARM and need "@0".
+ */
+static u32 cpu_comm_parse_id(char *token)
+{
+	unsigned int cpu = CPU_COMM_CPU_MIPS;
+	char *at;
+	u32 id;
+
+	at = strchr(token, '@');
+	if (at) {
+		*at++ = '\0';
+		if (kstrtouint(at, 0, &cpu))
+			return 0;
+	}
+
+	if (!strncmp(token, "0x", 2) || !strncmp(token, "0X", 2)) {
+		if (kstrtou32(token, 16, &id))
+			return 0;
+		return id;
+	}
+
+	return cpu_comm_name2id(token, cpu);
+}
+
+static void cpu_comm_debug_cb(u32 comp_id, const u32 *args,
+			      unsigned int nargs, void *ctx)
+{
+	char buf[96];
+	int n = 0;
+	unsigned int i;
+
+	buf[0] = '\0';
+	for (i = 0; i < nargs && n < (int)sizeof(buf) - 12; i++)
+		n += scnprintf(buf + n, sizeof(buf) - n, "%s0x%08x",
+			       i ? " " : "", args[i]);
+
+	pr_info("cpu_comm: kernel callback comp=0x%08x nargs=%u args=[%s]\n",
+		comp_id, nargs, buf);
+}
+
+static ssize_t cpu_comm_debug_call_write(struct file *file,
+					 const char __user *ubuf,
+					 size_t len, loff_t *ppos)
+{
+	char kbuf[160], *cur, *tok;
+	u32 args[CPU_COMM_MAX_ARGS];
+	u32 res[CPU_COMM_MAX_ARGS];
+	unsigned int nargs = 0;
+	u32 comp_id;
+	int ret, n, i;
+
+	if (len == 0 || len >= sizeof(kbuf))
+		return -EINVAL;
+	if (copy_from_user(kbuf, ubuf, len))
+		return -EFAULT;
+	kbuf[len] = '\0';
+
+	cur = strim(kbuf);
+	tok = strsep(&cur, " \t\n");
+	if (!tok || !*tok)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	comp_id = cpu_comm_parse_id(tok);
+	if (!comp_id)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	while ((tok = strsep(&cur, " \t\n")) != NULL) {
+		if (!*tok)
+			continue;
+		if (nargs >= CPU_COMM_MAX_ARGS)
+			return -E2BIG;
+		if (kstrtou32(tok, 0, &args[nargs]))
+			return -EINVAL;
+		nargs++;
+	}
+
+	ret = cpu_comm_call(comp_id, args, nargs, res, CPU_COMM_MAX_ARGS, 0);
+
+	mutex_lock(&cpu_comm_debug_mutex);
+	if (ret < 0) {
+		scnprintf(cpu_comm_debug_result, sizeof(cpu_comm_debug_result),
+			  "comp=0x%08x nargs=%u -> error %d\n",
+			  comp_id, nargs, ret);
+	} else {
+		n = scnprintf(cpu_comm_debug_result,
+			      sizeof(cpu_comm_debug_result),
+			      "comp=0x%08x nargs=%u -> ok, %d value(s)",
+			      comp_id, nargs, ret);
+		for (i = 0; i < ret; i++)
+			n += scnprintf(cpu_comm_debug_result + n,
+				       sizeof(cpu_comm_debug_result) - n,
+				       " 0x%08x", res[i]);
+		scnprintf(cpu_comm_debug_result + n,
+			  sizeof(cpu_comm_debug_result) - n, "\n");
+	}
+	pr_info("cpu_comm: debugfs call %s", cpu_comm_debug_result);
+	mutex_unlock(&cpu_comm_debug_mutex);
+
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+
+	*ppos += len;
+	return len;
+}
+
+static ssize_t cpu_comm_debug_call_read(struct file *file, char __user *ubuf,
+					size_t len, loff_t *ppos)
+{
+	ssize_t ret;
+
+	mutex_lock(&cpu_comm_debug_mutex);
+	ret = simple_read_from_buffer(ubuf, len, ppos, cpu_comm_debug_result,
+				      strlen(cpu_comm_debug_result));
+	mutex_unlock(&cpu_comm_debug_mutex);
+	return ret;
+}
+
+static const struct file_operations cpu_comm_debug_call_fops = {
+	.owner	= THIS_MODULE,
+	.open	= simple_open,
+	.read	= cpu_comm_debug_call_read,
+	.write	= cpu_comm_debug_call_write,
+	.llseek	= default_llseek,
+};
+
+/*
+ * "MipsHalCallback_SignalChange" installs the logging handler above for
+ * that routine, descriptor and all; a leading '-' removes it again. Only
+ * a name is accepted, not a raw id: the descriptor the co-processor looks
+ * the callback up by carries the name, and there is no way back from the
+ * hash to it.
+ */
+static ssize_t cpu_comm_debug_watch_write(struct file *file,
+					  const char __user *ubuf,
+					  size_t len, loff_t *ppos)
+{
+	char kbuf[96], *tok;
+	bool remove = false;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (len == 0 || len >= sizeof(kbuf))
+		return -EINVAL;
+	if (copy_from_user(kbuf, ubuf, len))
+		return -EFAULT;
+	kbuf[len] = '\0';
+	tok = strim(kbuf);
+
+	if (*tok == '-') {
+		remove = true;
+		tok++;
+	}
+	if (!*tok)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	if (remove)
+		ret = cpu_comm_unregister_callback(tok, cpu_comm_debug_cb);
+	else
+		ret = cpu_comm_register_callback(tok, cpu_comm_debug_cb, NULL);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	*ppos += len;
+	return len;
+}
+
+#define CPU_COMM_WATCH_BUF	2048
+
+/*
+ * Everything needed to say where an expected callback got stuck, in one
+ * read:
+ *
+ *   rx_calls	 how many MIPS->ARM calls reached cpu_comm_kernel_deliver()
+ *		 at all. Zero while the firmware logs the callback means the
+ *		 call is not on the wire -- look at the routine table, not
+ *		 at the handler.
+ *   unmatched	 of those, how many no handler claimed, and the id of the
+ *		 last one. Non-zero means calls arrive and are dropped here.
+ *   channel	 the key Comm_Add2NewCallFifo() looks up for kernel
+ *		 callbacks, and whether it is in the pool.
+ *   one line per handler: id, events delivered to it, what the shared
+ *		 routine table says about the id ("rt none" is the state
+ *		 in which the firmware cannot find the receiver), and the
+ *		 name the descriptor was built from.
+ */
+static ssize_t cpu_comm_debug_watch_read(struct file *file, char __user *ubuf,
+					 size_t len, loff_t *ppos)
+{
+	unsigned long channel = 0;
+	bool have_pool;
+	char *buf;
+	int i, n = 0;
+	ssize_t ret;
+
+	buf = kzalloc(CPU_COMM_WATCH_BUF, GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!buf)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	have_pool = pcpu_comm_dev &&
+		    Comm_QueryChannel(cpu_comm_channel_pool(),
+				      CPU_COMM_KERNEL_CHANNEL, &channel) == 0;
+
+	mutex_lock(&cpu_comm_kcb_mutex);
+
+	n += scnprintf(buf + n, CPU_COMM_WATCH_BUF - n,
+		       "rx_calls  %u\nunmatched %u last 0x%08x\n"
+		       "channel   0x%08x %s\nhandlers  %u\n",
+		       atomic_read(&cpu_comm_rx_calls),
+		       atomic_read(&cpu_comm_rx_unmatched),
+		       cpu_comm_rx_last_unmatched,
+		       (u32)CPU_COMM_KERNEL_CHANNEL,
+		       (have_pool && channel) ? "registered" : "missing",
+		       atomic_read(&cpu_comm_kcb_count));
+
+	for (i = 0; i < CPU_COMM_MAX_KERNEL_CB; i++) {
+		char rt[48];
+		u16 target_cpu = 0;
+		u32 owner = 0;
+
+		if (!cpu_comm_kcbs[i].fn)
+			continue;
+
+		if (cpu_comm_routine_owner(cpu_comm_kcbs[i].comp_id,
+					   &owner, &target_cpu))
+			scnprintf(rt, sizeof(rt), "rt none");
+		else
+			scnprintf(rt, sizeof(rt), "rt cpu=%u owner=%u%s",
+				  target_cpu, owner,
+				  owner == CPU_COMM_KERNEL_OWNER ? "" : " (foreign)");
+
+		n += scnprintf(buf + n, CPU_COMM_WATCH_BUF - n,
+			       "0x%08x %6lu %-28s %s\n",
+			       cpu_comm_kcbs[i].comp_id,
+			       cpu_comm_kcbs[i].hits, rt,
+			       cpu_comm_kcbs[i].name);
+	}
+	mutex_unlock(&cpu_comm_kcb_mutex);
+
+	ret = simple_read_from_buffer(ubuf, len, ppos, buf, n);
+	kfree(buf);
+	return ret;
+}
+
+static const struct file_operations cpu_comm_debug_watch_fops = {
+	.owner	= THIS_MODULE,
+	.open	= simple_open,
+	.read	= cpu_comm_debug_watch_read,
+	.write	= cpu_comm_debug_watch_write,
+	.llseek	= default_llseek,
+};
+
+void cpu_comm_api_init(void)
+{
+	if (cpu_comm_debug_dir)
+		return;
+
+	cpu_comm_debug_dir = debugfs_create_dir("cpu_comm", NULL);
+	debugfs_create_file("call", 0600, cpu_comm_debug_dir, NULL,
+			    &cpu_comm_debug_call_fops);
+	debugfs_create_file("watch", 0600, cpu_comm_debug_dir, NULL,
+			    &cpu_comm_debug_watch_fops);
+}
+
+void cpu_comm_api_exit(void)
+{
+	debugfs_remove_recursive(cpu_comm_debug_dir);
+	cpu_comm_debug_dir = NULL;
+
+	cpu_comm_drop_all_handlers();
+}
+
+#else /* !CONFIG_DEBUG_FS */
+
+void cpu_comm_api_init(void) { }
+
+void cpu_comm_api_exit(void)
+{
+	cpu_comm_drop_all_handlers();
+}
+
+#endif /* CONFIG_DEBUG_FS */
